ZERO STUDIO / ZERO OS GUEST EDITOR

Implementation
All editor implementation is original Zero. Ezero is a selectable input language
for the bundled compiler. No foreign editor, GUI toolkit, shell command, or host
compiler is launched by the guest editor. The window manager paints the canvas
with Zero's own font and pixel renderer.

Open
Type edit, or edit FILE. A missing named file opens an empty, unsaved buffer;
opening the editor does not create or modify a file. Paths use the current
volume, or the existing A:|NAME form. The selected filesystem service validates
names, capacity and write permission. A failed open keeps the current buffer.

Keys
F6 saves. An untitled buffer first asks for a filename.
F7 opens a filename dialog. Missing files become new unsaved buffers.
F8 compiles and runs the source with the installed Zero or Ezero toolchain.
F9 selects Zero or Ezero. The selected language is visible in the title.
F10 starts a new buffer. Esc closes the editor.
Arrow keys move the caret; Home and End move within a line; Backspace and Delete
remove text. Enter inserts a line break. Tab inserts one space.
Unsaved changes require Y before closing, opening another file or starting a
new buffer. N or Esc cancels the discard. Merely choosing an action does not
write the buffer. Ctrl+S/O/B/L/N are equivalent serial input events.

Limits in this first guest editor
The writable guest file service currently limits source to 400 ASCII bytes and
filenames to 31 characters. The editor checks this before insertion and does not
silently truncate. It rejects binary/non-ASCII files and files over 400 bytes.
Opening a CRLF file normalizes line endings in memory; disk bytes change only
on Save. The display uses ten code rows with line numbers beginning at 1 and an
insertion caret. Long source lines wrap; the viewport follows the caret.
The diagnostic line shows the first 80 characters. The editor also retains up
to 1023 output bytes, and mirrors a bounded status/output excerpt to serial.
The editor's editing controls are keyboard driven. The system mouse pointer
is separately implemented by the Zero PS/2 driver.

Build and files
F8 uses the same validated compiler bundle as the terminal zero and ezero
commands. Compilation failures leave the source intact. Running a program has
the same current guest runtime/file-service capabilities as terminal programs;
this first editor is not a process sandbox or debugger.
Open and Save use the actual cooperative guest file service with an independent
continuation. Save reports success only after that service returns success.
The editor state is outside the compiler workspace and filesystem buffers.

Integration
The editor is modal type 3 at console+2416. Its 64KiB private region is
network_base+163840, shared exclusively with the inactive browser. It uses the
first 8192 bytes: state, filename fields,400-byte source, diagnostics, a temporary
read buffer, bounded serial status and an 80-column by 16-row frame at+4096.
File-service continuations use the existing compiler workspace descriptor and
owned child stack. Compilation uses state 760 and the existing compiler block
exactly once; its result returns to the editor. No editor memory touches the
user disk directly.
